              "text": "The short answer is **no, they are not automatically released.** In most legal systems, individuals convicted of drug offenses before legalization still have to serve their sentences. \n\nWhether an incarcerated person is released or has their sentence reduced depends entirely on **how the legalization law was written** and whether it includes provisions for **retroactive relief**.\n\nThe legal, practical, and political reasons behind how this works involve several key factors:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Principle of Non-Retroactivity\nIn general jurisprudence, laws are not retroactive. \n* Just as a government cannot arrest you today for doing something that was legal yesterday but is illegal today (a protection known as *ex post facto*), the reverse is also true. \n* When a drug is legalized, it does not retroactively erase past violations of the law. Under the legal logic of many countries, including the United States, the offender is being punished for **violating the law as it stood at the time of the offense**. \n\nThe U.S. is one of the few developed countries that does not automatically recognize **\"retroactive ameliorative relief\"** (the principle that if a law is made more lenient, past offenders should automatically benefit from the change). Therefore, unless lawmakers write retroactivity directly into a new law, prisoners remain incarcerated.\n\n### 2. Explicit Legislative Retroactivity (Expungement and Resentencing)\nBecause release is not automatic, lawmakers must intentionally design legalization bills to include **retroactive relief**. In recent years, as more jurisdictions have legalized recreational cannabis, there has been a growing push to include these provisions:\n\n* **Petition-Based Relief:** Some laws allow incarcerated individuals to petition a court to have their cases reviewed, their sentences reduced, or their convictions expunged. However, this is a slow, complex, and expensive legal process that places the burden on the prisoner to hire a lawyer and navigate the court system.\n* **Automatic Clean Slate Laws:** Some progressive legalization frameworks (such as in California and Illinois) feature automatic expungement and resentencing. In these states, the government is tasked with identifying eligible individuals, dismissing their cases, and releasing them or reducing their probation.\n\n### 3. The Charge Matters (Possession vs. Trafficking)\nWhen a recreational drug is legalized, the law typically only permits the possession of **small, personal-use amounts** and establishes a highly regulated, licensed market for sales. \n\nMost people serving long-term prison sentences for drug offenses are not there for simple possession; they are incarcerated for **distribution, manufacturing, trafficking, or possessing quantities far exceeding the new legal limits**. Because unlicensed, large-scale distribution remains illegal even after legalization, those convicted of trafficking rarely qualify for release under new legalization laws.\n\n### 4. Executive Clemency and Pardons\nIf the legalization law itself does not offer retroactive relief, the only remaining option for incarcerated individuals is executive clemency. \n* **Pardons and Commutations:** A president (at the federal level) or a governor (at the state level) can issue executive orders to commute (shorten) sentences or pardon individuals convicted of newly legalized offenses. \n* For example, U.S. President Joe Biden issued categorical pardons for federal simple possession of marijuana. However, because simple possession rarely carries federal prison time on its own, this act primarily cleared the records of past offenders rather than freeing active prisoners. \n\n### Summary\nIf a recreational drug is legalized, prisoners **do not automatically go free**. They must continue serving their sentences unless the legalizing legislation specifically includes retroactivity and resentencing clauses, or unless an executive authority (like a governor or president) grants them clemency.",
